  "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(0)=2,$ which gives the point $(0,2),$ and\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(2)=1,$ which gives the point $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14189776/assets/14ca58b7ec006bba9465672bb00da30e2539125f66a460ad5b5ea38521a836f5.png)",
